/**
  @page fem-ciarlet Ciarlet's definition of a finite element
  @brief Separate the reference cell, approximation space, and degrees of freedom.

  A finite element is a local construction. It specifies which functions can be
  represented on one cell and which measurements determine those functions.
  A @ref fem-spaces "finite element space" combines these local constructions
  over a mesh, with the required continuity between neighboring cells.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section fem-ciarlet-definition The finite element triple

  A finite element is a triple @f$(\widehat K,\widehat P,\widehat\Sigma)@f$:

  | Object | Role |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @f$\widehat K@f$ | Reference cell on which the local construction is defined |
  | @f$\widehat P@f$ | Finite-dimensional space of functions on that cell |
  | @f$\widehat\Sigma=\{\sigma_0,\ldots,\sigma_{N-1}\}@f$ | Linear functionals determining a function in @f$\widehat P@f$ |

  The functionals are the **degrees of freedom**. Depending on the element family,
  they measure point values, moments, normal components, or tangential components.
  Together with the reference cell and approximation space, they specify the
  finite element.

  @subsection fem-ciarlet-unisolvence Require unique reconstruction

  The functionals must be unisolvent on the approximation space. With
  @f$N=\dim\widehat P@f$, the map
  @f[
    \widehat p\longmapsto
      (\sigma_0(\widehat p),\ldots,\sigma_{N-1}(\widehat p))
  @f]
  must be an isomorphism. Equivalently, the only function with every DOF equal
  to zero is the zero function. This condition gives a dual shape-function basis
  @f$\{\widehat\phi_i\}@f$ satisfying
  @f[
    \sigma_j(\widehat\phi_i)=\delta_{ij},\qquad
    \widehat p=\sum_i\sigma_i(\widehat p)\widehat\phi_i.
  @f]

  @section fem-ciarlet-p1 A linear triangle

  On the reference triangle with vertices @f$(0,0)@f$, @f$(1,0)@f$, and
  @f$(0,1)@f$, choose affine polynomials and vertex evaluations. The three basis
  functions are
  @f[
    \widehat\phi_0(r,s)=1-r-s,\qquad
    \widehat\phi_1(r,s)=r,\qquad
    \widehat\phi_2(r,s)=s.
  @f]
  Each is one at its own vertex and zero at the other two. Their sum is one,
  so constant fields are reproduced exactly.

  @image html Images/P1Basis.png "P1 triangle basis functions evaluated by Rodin. Color shows each basis value on the reference cell." width=720px

  The figure is generated from the reference-element evaluations in this
  runnable example:

  @snippet Variational/FiniteElementSpaces.cpp spaces-reference

  The reference cell is used here, so the sampling coordinates are reference
  coordinates. A value evaluated on a physical mesh cell must also use that
  cell's geometric mapping.

  @section fem-ciarlet-polynomials Match the approximation space to the cell

  On a simplex, total-degree polynomials have
  @f[
    \dim\mathbb P_k(\mathbb R^d)=\binom{k+d}{d}.
  @f]
  For a triangle, this gives three scalar DOFs at degree one and six at degree
  two. On a tetrahedron it gives four and ten, respectively.

  Tensor-product cells use a different construction. For a quadrilateral,
  @f$\mathbb Q_k@f$ contains polynomials of degree at most @f$k@f$ in each
  reference coordinate and has @f$(k+1)^2@f$ DOFs. A bilinear quadrilateral therefore has four vertex DOFs. The element reference
  specifies the approximation space for each cell family.

  @section fem-ciarlet-global Map the element to physical cells

  Let @f$F_K:\widehat K\to K@f$ map the reference cell to a physical cell.
  Scalar basis functions are transported by composition:
  @f[
    \phi_i^K=\widehat\phi_i\circ F_K^{-1}.
  @f]
  For an invertible square Jacobian @f$J_K=DF_K@f$,
  @f[
    \nabla_x\phi_i^K=J_K^{-T}\widehat\nabla\widehat\phi_i,
    \qquad dx=|\det J_K|\,d\widehat x.
  @f]
  Embedded surfaces instead have rectangular Jacobians and use the induced
  metric and tangential gradient. The @ref examples-pdes-surfacemesh "surface
  PDE tutorial" illustrates that setting.

  Polynomial degree is a property of the reference approximation space.
  Under a non-affine geometry map, the physical basis is the composition of a
  reference polynomial with the inverse map. Field degree and geometry degree are
  selected independently.

  @section fem-ciarlet-conformity Assemble a global space

  Local DOFs on shared mesh entities are identified when the chosen continuity
  requires it. Continuous nodal spaces share vertex values and, at higher degree,
  the appropriate edge and face data. Discontinuous spaces retain independent
  cell data.

  | Conformity | Intercell requirement | Examples in this guide |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | @f$H^1@f$ | Compatible continuous traces | `P1`, `H1` |
  | @f$L^2@f$ only | No intercell continuity imposed | `P0` |
  | @f$H(\mathrm{div})@f$ | Compatible normal traces | Requires an appropriate vector element family |
  | @f$H(\mathrm{curl})@f$ | Compatible tangential traces | Requires an appropriate vector element family |

  The last two conformity classes require element families with the corresponding
  normal or tangential trace DOFs; the table identifies their mathematical
  requirements.

  @section fem-ciarlet-rodin Locate the objects in Rodin

  `Polytope::Type` identifies a reference geometry. Element classes such as
  @ref Rodin::Variational::P1Element "P1Element" provide local basis evaluations
  and DOF functionals. Space objects distribute DOFs over a mesh, while
  @ref Rodin::Variational::GridFunction "GridFunction" represents one discrete
  field in that space.

  @snippet Variational/FiniteElementSpaces.cpp spaces-setup

  This setup defines several spaces on the same mesh for comparison.
  The @ref fem-spaces "finite element spaces guide" checks their global counts,
  assigns a known function, and evaluates the interpolated fields. See also the
  @ref guides-polytopes "polytope guide" and
  @ref guides-numerical-contracts "numerical contracts".
*/
